## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a practical data structure solution for maintaining top-K items under batch updates, balancing per-update cost and output cost, and comparing against naive approaches.

## Key Concepts
- **Top-K maintenance** with dynamic count updates
- **Batch amortization** — distributing work across batch items rather than per-event
- **Heap with lazy updates** — using a min-heap for efficient top-K tracking
- **Space-time trade-offs** — comparing near-linear space with output efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopKTracker {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap(); // stores (count, id) pairs
  }

  processBatch(updates) {
    // Phase 1: Apply all deltas to hashmap
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const newCount = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + delta;
      this.counts.set(id, newCount);
    }

    // Phase 2: Reconcile heap with updated counts
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const count = this.counts.get(id);
      if (this.heap.has(id)) {
        this.heap.update(id, count);
      } else if (this.heap.size < this.k) {
        this.heap.insert(id, count);
      } else if (count > this.heap.peekMin().count) {
        this.heap.replaceMin(id, count);
      }
    }
  }

  getTopK() {
    return this.heap.getAllSorted(); // returns K items sorted by count descending
  }
}
```

## Amortized Analysis
Each batch processes B events. Updating the hashmap costs O(B) total. Each heap operation costs O(log K), and at most B such operations occur per batch, yielding O(B log K) amortized time. Since B << N in practice (batch sizes are typically much smaller than total unique keys), this significantly outperforms re-scanning all N items after every batch, which costs O(N log N) per batch.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of dynamic connectivity under incremental edge insertions and practical amortized analysis in graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts

- Disjoint Set Union (DSU) with path compression and union by size
- Amortized α(n) (inverse Ackermann function) for near-constant operations
- Batch processing efficiency vs full recomputation approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.components = n;
  }

  find(a) {
    if (this.parent[a] !== a) {
      this.parent[a] = this.find(this.parent[a]);
    }
    return this.parent[a];
  }

  union(a, b) {
    const rootA = this.find(a);
    const rootB = this.find(b);
    if (rootA === rootB) return false;

    if (this.size[rootA] < this.size[rootB]) {
      [rootA, rootB] = [rootB, rootA];
    }
    this.parent[rootB] = rootA;
    this.size[rootA] += this.size[rootB];
    this.components--;
    return true;
  }
}

function processBatches(n, batches) {
  const dsu = new DSU(n);
  const results = [];

  for (const batch of batches) {
    for (const [u, v] of batch) {
      dsu.union(u, v);
    }
    results.push(dsu.components);
  }

  return results;
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing:** O(n) time to initialize DSU arrays, O(n) memory for parent and size arrays.

**Per-batch updates:** O(B·α(n)) time for B union operations with path compression and union by size.

**Total complexity:** O(n + m·α(n)) time, O(n) memory.

**Comparison to recomputation:** Full DFS/BFS after each batch would cost O(n + m) per batch, totaling O((n + m)·(m/B)) time—significantly worse for large graphs with many small batches.

The DSU approach leverages amortized near-constant operations, making it ideal for dynamic connectivity scenarios with incremental updates.
